1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,169
A service is down, and people are calling.

2
00:00:03,189 --> 00:00:07,087
Check it, start it, make sure it comes back after a reboot.

3
00:00:07,107 --> 00:00:09,809
Three small jobs, one command: systemctl.

4
00:00:10,279 --> 00:00:14,569
In this video: start, stop, and check the status of network services.

5
00:00:19,089 --> 00:00:21,672
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:25,386
Start, stop, and check the status of network services.

7
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:30,215
We'll see which services listen on the network, check them, stop and start one,

8
00:00:30,235 --> 00:00:32,044
and control what happens at boot.

9
00:00:41,564 --> 00:00:46,211
A network service is a program that waits for connections on a port.

10
00:00:46,231 --> 00:00:50,397
systemd manages them, and you talk to systemd with systemctl.

11
00:00:56,867 --> 00:01:01,161
There are two separate questions. Is it running right now? That is active.

12
00:01:01,181 --> 00:01:05,999
And will it start at boot? That is enabled. A service can be one without the other.

13
00:01:14,469 --> 00:01:19,036
The verbs are simple: status, start, stop, restart, enable, disable.

14
00:01:24,556 --> 00:01:27,644
First: what is listening? ss shows sockets.

15
00:01:27,664 --> 00:01:33,354
Dash t is tcp, dash l is listening only, dash n shows numbers instead of names.

16
00:01:33,374 --> 00:01:37,763
We see port twenty-two, which is ssh, and port ninety-ninety.

17
00:01:46,233 --> 00:01:51,311
Add dash p, with sudo, and ss also shows which program owns each port.

18
00:01:51,331 --> 00:01:53,642
Port twenty-two belongs to sshd.

19
00:01:53,662 --> 00:01:56,919
Port ninety-ninety is held by systemd itself,

20
00:01:56,939 --> 00:02:00,634
which starts the web console when someone connects.

21
00:02:12,154 --> 00:02:15,965
Now the status. systemctl status prints a full report.

22
00:02:15,985 --> 00:02:20,079
The pipe and head dash n three keep the first three lines:

23
00:02:20,099 --> 00:02:24,974
the name, whether it is loaded and enabled, and whether it is active.

24
00:02:24,994 --> 00:02:26,961
sshd is enabled and running.

25
00:02:37,431 --> 00:02:40,416
For scripts, there are two short questions.

26
00:02:40,436 --> 00:02:43,981
is-active says active, and is-enabled says enabled.

27
00:02:50,451 --> 00:02:55,573
We repeat the status for chronyd, the time service. It is also enabled and running.

28
00:03:03,093 --> 00:03:07,255
Now we stop chronyd. It needs sudo, and it prints nothing.

29
00:03:07,275 --> 00:03:11,941
is-active now says inactive. Never stop sshd on a remote machine:

30
00:03:11,961 --> 00:03:14,465
you would lose your own connection.

31
00:03:22,935 --> 00:03:24,986
systemctl start brings it back.

32
00:03:25,006 --> 00:03:27,926
And restart stops and starts it in one step,

33
00:03:27,946 --> 00:03:31,534
which is what you do after changing its configuration.

34
00:03:43,054 --> 00:03:44,754
Now the boot behaviour.

35
00:03:44,774 --> 00:03:50,212
systemctl disable removes the link that starts the service at boot. Look:

36
00:03:50,232 --> 00:03:56,492
is-enabled says disabled, but is-active still says active. Disable does not stop it.

37
00:03:56,512 --> 00:03:58,735
It only changes the next boot.

38
00:04:08,255 --> 00:04:11,691
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

39
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:18,033
We reboot. Afterwards chronyd is disabled and inactive, as predicted.

40
00:04:18,053 --> 00:04:19,647
One command fixes both:

41
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:24,839
enable dash dash now enables it for the next boot and starts it right now.

42
00:04:34,359 --> 00:04:36,603
Your turn. Stop and disable chronyd.

43
00:04:36,623 --> 00:04:41,507
Then enable it and start it again in one command. Reboot, and run the checker.

44
00:04:47,977 --> 00:04:49,728
Try before you open the solution.

45
00:04:55,248 --> 00:04:58,364
Next up: securely transfer files between systems.

46
00:04:58,834 --> 00:05:01,737
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
